   <dc:creator>
    <rdf:Seq>
     <rdf:li>Sofia Gonzalez-Sancho</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>Tom Ferrasse and Maria-Dolores Cano</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Seq>
   </dc:creator>
   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">[-15]Securing Locally Deployed LLM-RAG Assistants: A Cross-Model and Cross-Lingual Evaluation of Indirect Prompt Injection Defenses</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">[-15]Securing Locally Deployed LLM-RAG Assistants: A Cross-Model and Cross-Lingual Evaluation of Indirect Prompt Injection Defenses</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>large language models; retrieval-augmented generation; indirect prompt injection; edge intelligence; AI security; local LLMs; cross-lingual security; trustworthy AI</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) exposes locally deployed Large Language Model (LLM) assistants to indirect prompt injection through untrusted documents. This study evaluates four attack families---instruction override</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) exposes locally deployed Large Language Model (LLM) assistants to indirect prompt injection through untrusted documents. This study evaluates four attack families---instruction override</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">context contamination</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">context contamination</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">tool abuse</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">tool abuse</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and simulated data exfiltration---across five defense configurations (C1--C5) and four local LLMs</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and simulated data exfiltration---across five defense configurations (C1--C5) and four local LLMs</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">using a synthetic corpus of 40 documents and 32 queries. A complementary Spanish--English 2 $\times $ 2 experiment with a common multilingual retriever examines cross-lingual attack behavior. In the original Llama 3.1 8B experiment</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">using a synthetic corpus of 40 documents and 32 queries. A complementary Spanish--English 2 $\times $ 2 experiment with a common multilingual retriever examines cross-lingual attack behavior. In the original Llama 3.1 8B experiment</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">conditional Attack Success Rate (ASR) decreased from 70.4% without defenses (C1) to 22.2% with deterministic post-generation filtering (C5). Across the four models</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">conditional Attack Success Rate (ASR) decreased from 70.4% without defenses (C1) to 22.2% with deterministic post-generation filtering (C5). Across the four models</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">C5 achieved post-filter ASRs of 7.4--22.2%</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">C5 achieved post-filter ASRs of 7.4--22.2%</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">with no observed consequential tool misuse or concrete synthetic-data leakage</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">with no observed consequential tool misuse or concrete synthetic-data leakage</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">although Answer Utility ranged from 21.9% to 62.5%. Across the four Spanish--English language conditions</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">although Answer Utility ranged from 21.9% to 62.5%. Across the four Spanish--English language conditions</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">C5 post-filter ASR ranged from 14.3% to 40.7%; in the English--English condition</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">C5 post-filter ASR ranged from 14.3% to 40.7%; in the English--English condition</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the Spanish-calibrated filter did not remove English override phrases or disclosure structures</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the Spanish-calibrated filter did not remove English override phrases or disclosure structures</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and C5 did not improve on the model-enforced defense C4. Because the filter and automatic evaluator share some detection indicators</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and C5 did not improve on the model-enforced defense C4. Because the filter and automatic evaluator share some detection indicators</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">post-filter ASR reflects unsafe content remaining in delivered responses rather than intrinsic model resistance. All automatic labels were validated through adjudication</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">post-filter ASR reflects unsafe content remaining in delivered responses rather than intrinsic model resistance. All automatic labels were validated through adjudication</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">bilingual re-labeling</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">bilingual re-labeling</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and a blind semantic review of pre- and post-filter responses. Context contamination remained the main residual weakness. These findings support independent security enforcement while highlighting retrieval effects</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and a blind semantic review of pre- and post-filter responses. Context contamination remained the main residual weakness. These findings support independent security enforcement while highlighting retrieval effects</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">security--utility trade-offs</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">security--utility trade-offs</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and limitations of the controlled synthetic setting.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and limitations of the controlled synthetic setting.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
